Level","kind":{"Struct":[]},"layout":[{"key":"x86_64-unknown-linux-gnu","value":{"size":0,"align":1,"discriminator":{"Known":0},"uninhabited":false,"variant_layouts":[{"field_offsets":[],"uninhabited":false,"tagger":[]}],"repr":{"repr_algo":"Rust","align_modif":null,"transparent":false,"explicit_discr_type":false}}}],"ptr_metadata":"None"},{"def_id":11,"item_meta":{"name":[{"Ident":["generic_array",0]},{"Ident":["GenericArray",0]}],"span":{"data":{"file_id":12,"beg":{"line":179,"col":0},"end":{"line":179,"col":45}},"generated_from_span":null},"source_text":null,"attr_info":{"attributes":[{"DocComment":" Struct representing a generic array - `GenericArray<T, N>` works like [T; N]"}],"inline":null,"rename":null,"public":true},"is_local":false,"opacity":"Foreign","lang_item":null},"generics":{"regions":[],"types":[{"index":0,"name":"T"},{"index":1,"name":"U"},{"index":2,"name":"Clause0_ArrayType"}],"const_generics":[],"trait_clauses":[],"regions_outlive":[],"types_outlive":[],"trait_type_constraints":[]},"src":"TopLevel","kind":"Opaque","layout":[],"ptr_metadata":"None"},null,null,{"def_id":14,"item_meta":{"name":[{"Ident":["core",0]},{"Ident":["marker",0]},{"Ident":["PhantomData",0]}],"span":{"data":{"file_id":19,"beg":{"line":811,"col":0},"end":{"line":811,"col":39}},"generated_from_span":null},"source_text":null,"attr_info":{"attributes":[{"DocComment":" Zero-sized type used to mark things that \"act like\" they own a `T`."},{"DocComment":""},{"DocComment":" Adding a `PhantomData<T>` field to your type tells the compiler that your"},{"DocComment":" type acts as though it stores a value of type `T`, even though it doesn't"},{"DocComment":" really. This information is used when computing certain safety properties."},{"DocComment":""},{"DocComment":" For a more in-depth explanation of how to use `PhantomData<T>`, please see"},{"DocComment":" [the Nomicon](../../nomicon/phantom-data.html)."},{"DocComment":""},{"DocComment":" # A ghastly note 👻👻👻"},{"DocComment":""},{"DocComment":" Though they both have scary names, `PhantomData` and 'phantom types' are"},{"DocComment":" related, but not identical. A phantom type parameter is simply a type"},{"DocComment":" parameter which is never used. In Rust, this often causes the compiler to"},{"DocComment":" complain, and the solution is to add a \"dummy\" use by way of `PhantomData`."},{"DocComment":""},{"DocComment":" # Examples"},{"DocComment":""},{"DocComment":" ## Unused lifetime parameters"},{"DocComment":""},{"DocComment":" Perhaps the most common use case for `PhantomData` is a struct that has an"},{"DocComment":" unused lifetime parameter, typically as part of some unsafe code. For"},{"DocComment":" example, here is a struct `Slice` that has two pointers of type `*const T`,"},{"DocComment":" presumably pointing into an array somewhere:"},{"DocComment":""},{"DocComment":" ```compile_fail,E0392"},{"DocComment":" struct Slice<'a, T> {"},{"DocComment":"     start: *const T,"},{"DocComment":"     end: *const T,"},{"DocComment":" }"},{"DocComment":" ```"},{"DocComment":""},{"DocComment":" The intention is that the underlying data is only valid for the"},{"DocComment":" lifetime `'a`, so `Slice` should not outlive `'a`. However, this"},{"DocComment":" intent is not expressed in the code, since there are no uses of"},{"DocComment":" the lifetime `'a` and hence it is not clear what data it applies"},{"DocComment":" to. We can correct this by telling the compiler to act *as if* the"},{"DocComment":" `Slice` struct contained a reference `&'a T`:"},{"DocComment":""},{"DocComment":" ```"},{"DocComment":" use std::marker::PhantomData;"},{"DocComment":""},{"DocComment":" # #[allow(dead_code)]"},{"DocComment":" struct Slice<'a, T> {"},{"DocComment":"     start: *const T,"},{"DocComment":"     end: *const T,"},{"DocComment":"     phantom: PhantomData<&'a T>,"},{"DocComment":" }"},{"DocComment":" ```"},{"DocComment":""},{"DocComment":" This also in turn infers the lifetime bound `T: 'a`, indicating"},{"DocComment":" that any references in `T` are valid over the lifetime `'a`."},{"DocComment":""},{"DocComment":" When initializing a `Slice` you simply provide the value"},{"DocComment":" `PhantomData` for the field `phantom`:"},{"DocComment":""},{"DocComment":" ```"},{"DocComment":" # #![allow(dead_code)]"},{"DocComment":" # use std::marker::PhantomData;"},{"DocComment":" # struct Slice<'a, T> {"},{"DocComment":" #     start: *const T,"},{"DocComment":" #     end: *const T,"},{"DocComment":" #     phantom: PhantomData<&'a T>,"},{"DocComment":" # }"},{"DocComment":" fn borrow_vec<T>(vec: &Vec<T>) -> Slice<'_, T> {"},{"DocComment":"     let ptr = vec.as_ptr();"},{"DocComment":"     Slice {"},{"DocComment":"         start: ptr,"},{"DocComment":"         end: unsafe { ptr.add(vec.len()) },"},{"DocComment":"         phantom: PhantomData,"},{"DocComment":"     }"},{"DocComment":" }"},{"DocComment":" ```"},{"DocComment":""},{"DocComment":" ## Unused type parameters"},{"DocComment":""},{"DocComment":" It sometimes happens that you have unused type parameters which"},{"DocComment":" indicate what type of data a struct is \"tied\" to, even though that"},{"DocComment":" data is not actually found in the struct itself. Here is an"},{"DocComment":" example where this arises with [FFI]. The foreign interface uses"},{"DocComment":" handles of type `*mut ()` to refer to Rust values of different"},{"DocComment":" types. We track the Rust type using a phantom type parameter on"},{"DocComment":" the struct `ExternalResource` which wraps a handle."},{"DocComment":""},{"DocComment":" [FFI]: ../../book/ch19-01-unsafe-rust.html#using-extern-functions-to-call-external-code"},{"DocComment":""},{"DocComment":" ```"},{"DocComment":" # #![allow(dead_code)]"},{"DocComment":" # trait ResType { }"},{"DocComment":" # struct ParamType;"},{"DocComment":" # mod foreign_lib {"},{"DocComment":" #     pub fn new(_: usize) -> *mut () { 42 as *mut () }"},{"DocComment":" #     pub fn do_stuff(_: *mut (), 